<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1465">1465</a>–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar was a Spanish conquistador who led the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from 1511 and became its first governor. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baracoa">Baracoa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de Cuba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatuey">Hatuey</a>, who resisted, was burnt in 1512. In 1519 he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> but failed to stop him acting on his own account.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar.jpg" alt="Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DiegoVelazquezCuellar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Velázquez's early life. He was born in Cuéllar, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, around 1465, served for a time in the Spanish military in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. In September 1493 he was one of 1,500 men who sailed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> on his second voyage, and he never returned to Spain. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> he gained the regard of Bartholomew Columbus and became a trusted lieutenant of the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a>. In July 1503 he put down a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> revolt at Jaragua and then founded Salvatierra de la Zabana and other towns. By 1511 he was one of the wealthiest men on the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>King Ferdinand instructed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus</a> to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in the hope of gold and Native labour, and Velázquez was chosen to lead the expedition, which he had to finance himself. He sailed in January 1511 with four ships and three hundred men, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> force led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatuey">Hatuey</a>, formerly a chief from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, resisted and was defeated after two months. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>, Hatuey was captured and burned alive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baracoa">Baracoa</a> was settled by August 1511, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panfilo-de-narvaez">Pánfilo de Narváez</a> became second in command.</p><p>In 1513 three expeditions went west; Narváez defeated a force of 2,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taínos</a> under the chief Caguax, the last significant resistance. By 1515 Velázquez had founded Bayamo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, Sancti Spiritus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, Camagüey and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, which became the capital.</p><p>He rewarded relatives and associates with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a>, and by 1522 the encomenderos controlled almost 3,000 Indian labourers. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-hernandez-de-cordoba-founder-of-nicaragua">Francisco Hernández de Córdoba</a> to the Yucatán in 1517 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a> in 1518. He then chose Cortés to lead a third expedition, but doubted him and tried to replace him. Cortés sailed in February 1519 with ten ships and about 500 men. When Velázquez sent Narváez against him in 1520 with about 1,000 men, Narváez was defeated and most of his force joined Cortés. Velázquez lost a large fortune, and Cuba was left depopulated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Colón</a> sent Alonso Zuazo in January 1521 to replace Velázquez, but Velázquez was restored in July 1523. In 1522 Carlos I recognised Cortés as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. Velázquez died on 11 or 12 June 1524 and was buried under the altar steps of the new cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. At his death he was said to be the richest Spaniard in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. He had founded towns that remain important and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> a centre of trade and a staging point for expeditions of conquest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</b> led the conquest of Cuba from 1511, founding Baracoa and other towns; the Taíno chief Hatuey, who resisted, was burnt in 1512.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-cuba">Captaincy General of Cuba</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cuéllar, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Santiago de Cuba, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.4,-4.31&amp;year=1503" aria-label="Cuéllar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4445 688 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4895 958h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4895 958h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.4,-4.31&amp;year=1503">What was here in 1503? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
